title: 304 Not Modified is treated as an empty success
updated: 2026-08-27T06:34:39Z
---

Role::HTTP croaks on status >= 400 and decodes everything else. 304 falls
into the second group: no body, so _request hands back undef - exactly what
a 204 returns.

The daemon answers 304 for a no-op state change:
  POST /containers/{id}/start   on a running container
  POST /containers/{id}/stop    on a stopped one
  POST /containers/{id}/pause   on a paused one, and unpause likewise

So $docker->containers->start($id) cannot tell "started it" from "it was
already running". Callers that care - anything idempotent, anything
reporting what it did - have no way to ask.

Decide the semantics explicitly and document it on each affected method:
either return a distinguishable value (the status code, or a small result
object), or croak, or state in the POD that 304 is folded into success on
purpose. Any of the three beats the current silence.

The Engine reference calls this out because clients get it wrong; see the
docker-engine-api skill, "Response shapes".


--- DECISION 2026-08-27 (maintainer) ---
304 becomes a changed-flag. start/stop/pause/unpause/restart return 1 when
the call changed something (204) and 0 when the container was already in the
target state (304). They return undef today; 0 and undef are both falsy, so
no caller that ignores the return value or tests it for truth changes
behaviour. Documented per method in the POD.
